t to be able to  think about designing these experiments  with  the types of reagents that are available  from a commercial partner such as bio  legend in this case  who've done the work to make sure that  the energy antibodies you're using  are at the right concentration before  you use them if you're using something  that's  novel to you and it's just developed  from hybridoma for example then  these are the kinds of experimental qc  steps you might want to consider  before embarking on this type of  experiment  okay but when you have all these  reagents in place you have the  dextromers conjugated with  oligonucleotide  and fluorophore that allows them also to  be sorted you have  a combination of fluorescently labeled  antibodies as well as oligonucleotide  tag feature barcode enabled antibody  reagents  you can think about adding these all to  cells according to a particular  experimental design  in this case we load the dextromers  first waited 10 minutes  added the antibody reagents and then  washed a number of times before we  started to sort them for a combination  of  cda positive as well as dextromer  positive t  cells and so the gaining strategy is  shown on the lower right hand side  and from pbmc's the starting material we  went through a series of different steps  including a dead cell removal  as jose mentioned in his presentation  some of the resources for this are shown  in the upper right-hand side but most of  this is captured in some  demonstrated protocols that we have  available on the 10x genomics website  and at the end of the day we start off  with a number of  gems a number of single cells we've  reduced that down to a really highly  selected number of  cd8 positive and dexamera positive t  cells so as jose pointed out  float cytometry in this case is  absolutely critical to the efficient  success of this type of experiment we're  looking for really really rare  dexterity positive and antigen specific  t cells in a cd8 positive population  specifically  and so if we started from just bulk  pvmcs we'd spend an incredible amount of  money just sorting pvmcs before we found  these little needles in the haystack  so flow cytometry ends up being a  critical uh part of this entire workflow  that's just shown here in in a broad  view again we're taking  single cells and a bunch of different  reagents performing a full cytometric  analysis  prior to putting those sorted cells into  the 10x genomics instrument  after which we can capture  simultaneously from the same single cell  the gene expression  mrna based signature as well as a  protein cell surface  epitope mapping signature those feature  barcode enabled  antibody reagents and again the t-cell  receptor gene sequences specifically  as well as the antigen specificity of  those same t  cells all from the same single cells  and in practice what that looks like is  shown here so  when we uh have in this particular  example used a cmv picture a positive  donor  and sorted out tbmcs from that  particular donor  we're able to sort cells according to  gene expression measurements at the mrna  level  and we're also able to define uh cell  sorting states  using the cell surface markers based on  the total seek antibody panel that we  had available  at the time and in those same single  cells we're able to resolve  rather nicely some very specific t cell  populations that  are phenotypically distinct in some  cases yet have the same  uh antigen specificities so shown on the  right hand side for example we have two  separate groups some c and b positive t  cells both  specific for hla or hlb of 0702  and a very specific cnb peptide and  separately we have another entire  clonally expanded set of tcr clinotypes  that are specific for  hlao101 and other cuv specific  peptides so within the same experimental  design we're able to resolve the t  cell receptor gene sequence the tcr  specificity for antigen  in presence of mac molecule as well as  the self-surface phenotype  the immunophenotype of that same t cell  when we scale this up we've done this a  couple different times at 10x genomics  we can think about  inputting millions of inputs cda  positive t cells  we can highly multiplex the number of  different antigens that we  test for specificity for in this  particular case we have  50 total dextrometer molecules all the  eight of those are negative controls we  ultimately  derived about 200 000 single cells after  sorting  and it took us about a week of time um  for the rest of the single cell mouse to  get that  selected down to a group of about 62  000 antigen binding cells that we are  comfortable calling antigen binding  which results and collapsed into about  11 600  separate tcr clonal specificities for  antigen  and shown in the final bottom right  portion of this is what happens when you  add this single experimental data set  from a single cell genomics experiment  to the existing database of uh tcr  energy specificity interactions  so that ends up leading us with 43 out  of 44 of the engines that we tested  having uh defined t cell receptor energy  specificity  which 25 of those energies had never  previously had a tcr specific  test specificity described in a database  before  so those are novel entries into the vga  database that we're pretty excited about  following up  in more detail at some point in the  future  so finally in summary just want to  review where we've come from throughout  the presentation  had a bit of a review on nutanix  genomics technology and before that with  jose  a review on how to think about designing  experiments that incorporate soil  cytometry into single cell genomic  separations and workflows  hopefully giving you some insight into  how to use the combination of flow  cytometry with multi-omex cytometry to  design the right  best experiment to think about how to  identify those rare cells and  and to define heterogeneity and we can  also think as  they point out about applications in  stem cell research not just in  immunology  and hopefully give you some food for  thought about how to think about a  holistic workflow from  sample acquisition all the way through  to bioinformatic analysis and this type  of workflow  and i'd like to thank uh the rest of the  organization who's not  standing with me here at this  presentation but at 10x genomics the  broad group of people  in um r d sales marketing other roles  that helped to  provide everything that we had a chance  to talk about today and i'd like to  thank you for your time and attention  would be happy to take some questions  and thank you jose and brian for that  informative presentation  and we will now start the live q a  portion of the webinar  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now  just simply click on the ask a question  box located on the far  left of your screen and we'll answer as  many questions as we have time for  so let's take a look at our first  incoming questions  because our audience is already sent us  several  our first question is  for brian did you notice a discrepancy  between rna and protein detection  for any of the genes  yeah thanks for that question we do see  that pretty commonly  in our customers as well it's a very  common  molecular genomic phenotype of looking  at rna and proteins simultaneously in  single cells and  i think we have biology to think for  this and it's very cell type dependent  obviously so  where rnas are expressed sometimes  proteins are not  terribly abundant and where proteins can  be stably expressed rnas can be  rapidly degraded so really what this  enables us to do is learn more about the  regulatory mechanisms that guide gene  expression both at the rna and protein  level  and so we think that integrating these  kinds of observations into our  world view of the multi  state of single cells was really  informative as we learn more about the  data and we see this  applied to more and more cells and an  organism  thank you so much brian and our next  question is for you jose  can epithelial cells be sorted out  selected for enriched for from a mixed  cell population and actually this is a  two-part question  what are the minimum input requirements  for example  can disassociated cells from core needle  biopsies  be recovered with the wolf system  yeah that's a good question uh obviously  it is a little bit loaded and it  obviously depends always on  uh the sample that you start with uh but  you know what you really minimally need  is to be able to have  single cell suspension uh sometimes  there's uh reagents to allow you to do  that before putting that into  uh the wolf cell sorter uh and in the  end you know what you want to be able to  have  is a minimal number of target cells that  you're going after  and in our hands i mean it really varies  but we've been able to get away with  the very kind of low end in the 10 to 20  000 cell  range of target cells that you sort out  sometimes you want to sort a little bit  more just so that you  you have enough in case you have lost  during pipetting and things like that  i hope that answered that question uh it  was a little bit multi-part thank you so  much jose and  and actually continuing the discussion  about sorting  how long would it take to sort about 10  000 cells with a wolf's order or  a different way we asked how many cells  could you process in  like let's say an hour yeah that's a  great question  uh it depends on the concentration we  give a guidance of the  concentration of cells that one can  start with  and in about 40 minutes uh you can  process about a milliliter of sample  um and so you know from from start to  finish  if you're doing a whole a mil sample it  could be about you know about an hour or  so  from setup and the concentrations there  uh  again range from 100 000 cells per ml to  a million cells per ml  um and and that just depends on the uh  the purity that you want to get at the  end on the lower c